                                "sense_id": "58826808-ae5e-3fd6-a2d1-b47c94f925af",
                                "text": "These were almost all written in Latin, which appears to have been almost a second tongue, used familiary in conversation, and still more familiary in composition."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "f512544f-f833-4772-82e4-6589fad2eedf",
                                "sense_id": "58826808-ae5e-3fd6-a2d1-b47c94f925af",
                                "text": "In this mansion the front room on the first floor was \"the office,\" in which, as I recollect it, my grandfather might generally be found seated in quiet dignity, reading or conversing with a visitor, who had come in familiary to see him and have a friendly chat."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "7fe1b80b-571f-449b-812e-37f6fa6e8005",
                                "sense_id": "58826808-ae5e-3fd6-a2d1-b47c94f925af",
                                "text": "One of the most successful business men of Harrisburg among the colored people is C. W. Strothers, familiary known as Colonel."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "9d452665-cb42-4ce0-a14a-dbd76f2a9cbe",
                                "sense_id": "58826808-ae5e-3fd6-a2d1-b47c94f925af",
                                "text": "In 1938, the Federal National Mortgage Association ( familiary known as Fannie Ma ) was created to fill this gap in the housing credit market."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "2a09a83b-715a-4a17-adb5-b18878867d3f",
                                "sense_id": "58826808-ae5e-3fd6-a2d1-b47c94f925af",
                                "text": "Main Bangl verbs are often pretty regular but the learner must, unlike English, be aware of the status of the subjects, as in whether the subject is referred formally or familiary or very familiary (which can also be used to derogate as well) though person or numbers do not impact on the verbs."
                            }
                        ]
